import ProjectionConstants.FourSix.Errata

/-!
# All sign matrices of order six

Let `𝒮₆` be the set of `6 × 6` sign matrices (`IsSignMatrix`) and `𝒟₆` the set of weights
`D = diag(w)` (`IsWeight w`); `√D S √D` is `weightedSign S w` and `Tr(AP)` is
`frobeniusInner A P`. We show `Tr(√D S √D P) ≤ 5/3` for every `S ∈ 𝒮₆`, every `D ∈ 𝒟₆` and every
orthogonal projection `P` (`frobeniusInner_weightedSign_le_five_thirds`). In particular
`kyFanSum 4 (√D S √D) ≤ 5/3`, which is the upper bound in `Π(4, 6) = 5/3`, where `Π(4, 6)` is
the notation of [JFA] for `maxRelProjConst ℝ 4 6` (see `ProjectionConstants.FourSix.Main`).

This replaces the reduction in [JFA, §4.4]. There, `Π(3, 6) < 5/3` and `Π(5, 6) ≤ 5/3` restrict
the problem to sign matrices of signature `(4, 2)`, and the classification of the two-graphs on
six vertices by Bussemaker, Mathon and Seidel reduces these to `A1, A2, A3`. Here we treat all
sixteen two-graphs on six vertices, and the classification is checked by the kernel.

The value `Tr(√D S √D P)` is invariant under switching `S ↦ ESE` (together with `P ↦ EPE`, see
`signSwitch`) and under relabelling the indices (together with `D` and `P`). We fix a
representative `twoGraphRep t` of each of the sixteen two-graphs: `A1, A2, A3` and thirteen
others (`otherTwoGraphs`). For `A1, A2, A3` the bound is `M₁ ≤ 5/3`, `M₂ ≤ 5/3`, `M₃ ≤ 5/3`
(`ProjectionConstants.FourSix.Errata`); for the others it is the certificate argument of
[JFA-E, item 4] with a rational matrix `C` (`otherCertificates`).

To see that the representatives cover every sign matrix, switch `S` so that its first row is
`(1, …, 1)`. The switched matrix is determined by the signs of the ten pairs `{a, c}` with
`1 ≤ a < c ≤ 5`, that is, by a code `b < 2¹⁰` (`normalizedPattern`, `codeOf`). For each of the
`1024` codes, `classificationTable` lists a sign vector `ε`, a permutation `σ` and an index `t`
with `twoGraphRep t = E (N_b)_σ E`, where `N_b` is the sign matrix of `b` and `E = diag(ε)`. The
kernel checks all entries (`checkTable_classificationTable`).

The table and the certificates were computed by a short program; only their verification is part
of the proof.

## Main definitions

* `otherTwoGraphs`, `twoGraphRep`: representatives of the sixteen two-graphs on six vertices.
* `otherCertificates`: the rational certificates (`SosCertificate`) for `otherTwoGraphs`.
* `normalizedPattern`, `codeOf`: the sign pattern `N_b` (first row `(1, …, 1)`) of a code `b`,
  and the code of a sign matrix with first row `(1, …, 1)`.
* `classificationTable`: for each code, the switching, permutation and representative.
* `checkEntry`, `checkTable`: the Boolean checks of the table, evaluated by the kernel.

## Main statements

* `frobeniusInner_weightedSign_twoGraphRep_le`: the bound for the sixteen representatives.
* `codeOf_spec`: a sign matrix with first row `(1, …, 1)` is the sign pattern of its code.
* `checkTable_classificationTable`: the classification, checked by the kernel.
* `exists_twoGraphRep_of_lt`: for every code `b < 2¹⁰`, `N_b` is equivalent to a representative
  under switching and relabelling.
* `frobeniusInner_weightedSign_le_five_thirds`: the bound for every sign matrix of order six.

## Implementation notes

Signs are encoded by Booleans (`true` stands for `-1`, see `boolSign`). The table stores the sign
vector `ε` and the representatives (`twoGraphRepMask`) as bit masks, and the permutation `σ` by
its octal digits (`octalDigit`), so that `decide +kernel` can check all `1024` entries.

## References

* [JFA] G. Basso, *Computation of maximal projection constants*, J. Funct. Anal. 277 (2019),
  arXiv:1901.07866.
* [JFA-E] G. Basso, *Erratum to "Computation of maximal projection constants"*,
  arXiv:2402.06672.
-/

